      "knowledge_point": "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/237c36808ded541ed264826866db3d2774dc2d5212f1292f21a6c25b3b07d0e1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down.",
      "worked_solution": "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($⌢$) over the interval $(1,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/a7d9436275984c12271f2c737eb005e1eaab5b308c2204040e7aff5e87faffb3.png)",
