      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/47b3d021487b88f0a335fc070bfd4846a0ea54dd137f6a0db165db5b27180d45.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)=0?$",
      "worked_solution": "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=c$ and $x=e$ are stationary points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/899cb5979c943b1d385f9015ffa57bf97d4a47b10fb2be4e653272ed16494d73.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=c$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=c$ only.",
